L["temple-boy"] = """Every year, Mary and Joseph traveled to Jerusalem for the Passover festival. When Jesus was twelve, they went up as usual. When the festival was over, they started the trip home. They traveled in a large group of relatives and neighbors, and the boy Jesus stayed behind in Jerusalem. His parents didn't realize it. Thinking he was somewhere in their group, they traveled for a day. Then they began looking for him among their relatives and friends.

When they didn't find him, they went back to Jerusalem to look for him. After three days, they found him in the temple courts. He was sitting among the teachers, listening to them and asking them questions. Everyone who heard him was amazed at his understanding and his answers.

When his parents saw him, they were astonished. His mother said to him, son, why have you treated us like this? Your father and I have been anxiously searching for you. Anyone who has lost track of a child for even a few minutes can feel what she felt.

Jesus answered with a question of his own. Why were you searching for me? Didn't you know I had to be in my Father's house? They did not understand what he was saying to them. This is the first recorded time that Jesus spoke, and he called God his Father.

Then he went down to Nazareth with them and was obedient to them. His mother treasured all these things in her heart. And Jesus grew in wisdom and stature, and in favor with God and man.

Why does this matter? It is the only story in the Gospels from Jesus' childhood. It shows that even at twelve, Jesus knew who he was and where he belonged. It also shows that he was a real child who grew up under human parents. He listened, he asked questions, and then he went home and obeyed them.

What happened after? Eighteen more years passed before Jesus began his public work. The Bible tells us almost nothing about those years, except that he grew up in Nazareth and worked as a carpenter like Joseph. When he was about thirty, he was baptized by John, and his ministry began.

What does this mean for us today? Parents can find comfort that even Mary and Joseph lost track of Jesus and had to go back and find him. It is also an invitation to ask questions about God. Jesus sat with teachers and asked honest questions. Faith doesn't require us to stop wondering. And it shows what to look for first. Jesus was in his Father's house, with his Father's word.

That is why this story is so important. It says that Jesus is the Son of God, who humbled himself to grow up like us. And it points to the day when he would again be in Jerusalem at Passover, doing his Father's work for the world."""
